"An American lives under two governments at the same time. One is the national government in Washington, usually called the federal government. The other is the government of the state where the person lives, with its own constitution, legislature, governor, and courts. Dividing power between two levels in this way is called federalism. It explains why the rate of sales tax, the requirements for a driver's license, and the punishment for many crimes change at a state line, while the dollar in a wallet, the stamp on a letter, and the rules for becoming a citizen are the same everywhere. In Madison's words, the two levels are “different agents and trustees of the people.”": "f27aa1154daa",
"An American lives under two governments at the same time. One is the national government in Washington, usually called the federal government. The other is the government of the state where the person lives, with its own constitution, legislature, governor, and courts. Dividing power between two levels in this way is called federalism. It explains why the rate of sales tax, the requirements for a driver's license, and the punishment for many crimes change at a state line, while the dollar in a wallet, the stamp on a letter, and the rules for becoming a citizen are the same everywhere. In Madison's words, the two levels are “different agents and trustees of the people.” State governments came first. When the Constitution was written, the thirteen states already had governments of their own, and few Americans wanted to see them swallowed by a distant capital. The Constitution therefore gave the national government a list of specific powers and left the rest where it was. James Madison put it plainly in The Federalist No. 45. The powers given to the national government, he wrote, “are few and defined,” while those that remain with the states “are numerous and indefinite.” The national government would deal mainly with matters such as war, peace, and foreign commerce, and the states with the ordinary affairs of daily life. Powers under the Constitution fall into a few groups. Enumerated powers are the ones listed for Congress, such as coining money, declaring war, and running the post office. Reserved powers are the ones left to the states by the Tenth Amendment, which says that powers not delegated to the United States “are reserved to the States respectively, or to the people.” Education, family law, and most criminal law belong here. Concurrent powers, such as taxing, borrowing, and running courts, are held by both levels at once. Finally, some powers are denied to the states outright. No state may coin money or make a treaty, and no state may tax imports without the consent of Congress. The boundary between the levels has never stayed still. In 1818 Maryland passed a law taxing a branch of the Bank of the United States, which Congress had created even though the Constitution says nothing about banks; the dispute reached the Supreme Court in 1819. In McCulloch v. Maryland, Chief Justice John Marshall upheld the bank under the clause that lets Congress make all laws “necessary and proper” for carrying out its listed powers, and he held that a state could not tax it, since “the power to tax involves the power to destroy.” Maryland had argued for reading the national government's powers narrowly. The Court read them to include the means of carrying them out, and held that a state may not obstruct a valid federal law. Two other tools have widened the national government's reach. The first is the Commerce Clause, which lets Congress regulate commerce “among the several States.” In Gibbons v. Ogden in 1824, the Supreme Court held that commerce includes navigation, so that steamboats licensed by Congress could not be shut out by a monopoly that New York had granted. Over time Congress has relied on that clause for a growing share of national laws, and how far it reaches is still argued. The second tool is money. Congress can attach conditions to the funds it sends the states. In 1984 Congress passed a law that withheld part of a state's federal highway funds unless it made twenty-one the minimum age for buying alcohol, and by 1988 every state had done so. Federalism has been used for good and ill. Before the Civil War, claims of “states' rights” were used to defend slavery, and later to defend racial segregation. Yet national power was also turned to slavery's ends: the Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 required the return of people who had escaped, and some Northern states passed laws to resist it. After the war, the Fourteenth Amendment forbade any state to deny a person “the equal protection of the laws.” In 1957, when the governor of Arkansas used the state's National Guard to keep Black students out of Central High School in Little Rock, President Eisenhower sent federal troops to enforce desegregation. The same division also lets states try ideas that the nation has not adopted. Justice Louis Brandeis wrote in 1932 that “a single courageous state may, if its citizens choose, serve as a laboratory; and try novel social and economic experiments without risk to the rest of the country.” History offers examples. Nine western states had adopted woman suffrage by 1912, years before the Nineteenth Amendment secured the vote for women across the country in 1920. Nebraska has run its legislature with a single chamber since 1937, while every other state has two. An idea that works in one state can be copied by others, and one that fails stays contained. The result is a country in which the answer to many questions depends on where you live. That variety is one of the purposes of the system, and it is also one of its costs. People who move from one state to another must learn new rules, businesses must follow fifty sets of laws, and a right may seem broader in one state than in the next. Americans have argued since 1787 about which matters should be settled close to home and which deserve one national standard, and the line has moved many times. The map in this lesson shows what that division looks like on a handful of questions that are not matters of party dispute.": "c5b5035f7b84",
